module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_fixture_names_units_its_declared_host_does_not_own

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_fixture_names_units_its_declared_host_does_not_own: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_fixture_names_units_its_declared_host_does_not_own" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "a hermetic world whose units are DERIVED from the host it declares instead of hand-written beside it: the withdrawal witness builds its slot world over the same host-grain population the route itself reads (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_retirement::host_drain_unit_scope, or the subject's own committed population), and states its premise in the open -- a fixture that finds its units are not the units that host owns fails LOUDLY, naming both the host and the units, rather than going red three layers down inside a refusal no one prints -- SUFFICIENT FOR a witness to red the merge path the next time a fixture's host and its unit names disagree, instead of that disagreement surfacing as eight unrelated-looking FAIL lines days after the authority moved",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**a world whose identity and whose units are hand-written next to each other, so nothing in the control can notice when they stop belonging together.** INVALID STATE: a hermetic fixture declares one host (its subject, its SshTarget, the host string it dials) while the units its world answers for, and the assertions that read that world, name units of a DIFFERENT host. Nothing about the fixture is internally inconsistent -- the unit names are well formed, the world answers them, the assertions match the world -- so the fixture resolves, typechecks and runs. What is false is the premise: the model's host-grain authority for the declared host does not cover those units, so the route under test refuses at its gate and never executes the legs the control exists to exercise. HARM, in three parts. First the signal is lost exactly when it is needed: a control suite that watched an authority change goes silent at the moment of the change, because the authority moving is what made the fixture's premise false. Second, the reds are unreadable: the claim dispatcher prints one PASS/FAIL line per control with no cause, so a fixture that refuses at its own gate looks identical to a route regression, and the cheapest way to make it green again is the WRONG one -- re-point the fixture at a host that happens to satisfy the new authority, or relax the assertion, and the disagreement survives to do the same damage to the next authority. Third, and worst, the survivors lie: the controls that still pass are precisely the ones that expect a refusal BEFORE the first leg runs, so a suite can show a third green and be cited in a PR body as evidence the bracket works while its actuation path is executed by none of them.",
    "SPECIMEN, 2026-11-20, dag/test/claim/runner/runner_host_withdraw_witness. The fixture declared subject host srv1 and a self-subject executor actions-runner@srv1-03 / srv1-13, while its world, its unit names and its record-content assertions carried actions-runner@srv3-01 and srv3-02. srv1 is a CI host modelled with five slots, actions-runner@srv1-01 through srv1-05, so the scope derived from the host the fixture declared contains NOT ONE of the units the fixture's world answers for. It stayed green while every permission the bracket consulted was host-agnostic. The cap bracket's drain permission then moved to HOST grain (gunbc.runner.runner_slot_retirement::host_drain_unit_scope, derived from the width authority, PR #16702 at a85fe5fc18ae), and behind it this one went unnoticed too: 8 of its 12 controls now sit RED ON MAIN, every one a control that needs the legs to actuate, while the 4 that pass assert a REFUSAL, or the ABSENCE of a file, so a route that never reaches a leg satisfies them without testing anything -- the three split-precondition controls by design, and the re-admission control through its 'no record was written' half. The two defects STACK: the fixture's five-word `systemctl show` answer had already made every read refuse (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_hand_built_fixture_shape_hides_the_shape_the_lowering_produces), and that read failure SHADOWED this one -- with host srv1 the scope the drain derives is actions-runner@srv1-01 through srv1-05, which names not one of the units the world answers for, so the stop leg would have had no permission even after the read was repaired. Repairing only the read leaves a fixture that still drains nothing and still looks plausible.",
    "WHAT CLOSES IT: derive the world from the host rather than writing the pair side by side, and make the disagreement observable at the fixture instead of inside the route's return value. Concretely for this witness: the slot world is built over the units the declared host's own grain yields (the drain scope, or the subject's committed population filtered to this fixture's world), the executor slot of a same-host control is taken from a host that actually holds executor slots, and a control that ends with no actuation at all says WHICH gate refused rather than comparing shapes. Concretely, this witness now carries a control asserting its own premise -- every unit in the fixture's world lies inside host_drain_unit_scope of the host the fixture declares -- and a second asserting that the world's `systemctl show` answer parses through the production reader. A ceiling check of that shape -- every hermetic host world's unit names a subset of the units its declared host owns, and every hand-written wire answer parses through the reader that consumes it -- would have caught both halves at the merges that moved the authorities, which is where the hours actually went.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
